    "terminal_reason": "completed",
    "result_text": "I implemented `publish_run` in `app/publish.py`, and all 3 tests pass (`pytest -q`). The tests use a fake API, so nothing has been uploaded to the real Hub yet.\n\n**The usual function isn't in this version.** The standard tool for very large folders is `upload_large_folder`, but it doesn't exist in the pinned huggingface-hub 2.0.0. In this version its behaviour is built into `upload_folder`. When the `hf_xet` package is available (1.6.0 is installed), `upload_folder`:\n- splits large folders into several commits, so the number of files won't make it fail;\n- resumes an interrupted upload when you run it again, skipping files already committed and not re-sending data that's already uploaded.\n\nI took this from the installed library's source (`hf_api.py`, `upload_folder`) rather than from online docs, since the installed code is what will actually run.\n\n**What `publish_run` does:**\n- Creates the dataset repo as private with `exist_ok=True`, so it's created the first time and reused after that.\n- Uploads the whole `run_dir` with `upload_folder` to the root of the repo.\n- Skips files matching `*.tmp` and `wandb/*`. A test confirms this drops `*.tmp` at any depth and everything under the top-level `wandb/`, but keeps files like `data/wandb.json`.\n- Takes an optional `api` argument, like `publish_summary` does.\n\n**One safeguard to know about:** without `hf_xet` (for example if `HF_HUB_DISABLE_XET` is set), `upload_folder` quietly falls back to a single huge commit. That can't resume and would likely fail on runs this big. So in that case `publish_run` raises a `RuntimeError` straight away instead of starting an upload that can't finish.\n\nI also added two tests to `tests/test_publish.py`: one checks the repo creation and upload call arguments, the other checks the skip patterns."
  },
